"The prognosis is influenced by several factors, including the preoperative PSA of 19 and the pathological T-stage of pT3a. Contributing to risk are positive surgical margins and extraprostatic extension. Countervailing findings include no seminal vesicle invasion and an absence of lymphovascular invasion. The lack of nodal sampling widens uncertainty regarding nodal status. Biopsy findings (Gleason 3+3, then 3+4) differ from the prostatectomy specimen's Gleason 3+4 (ISUP grade group 2), but both indicate adenocarcinoma. Prognostic revision would be significantly aided by postoperative PSA levels and their subsequent trajectory. The current data is limited by missing nodal sampling information. The fixed estimated horizon is 51.41 months. This represents expected follow-up without an event, not a known recurrence date. The sensitivity range is 33.42–69.40 months; its coverage has not been validated."
